#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447007} :activity/id #uuid "f05955d9-91c2-56df-a6bb-8701f2348c16" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049150 :topic/id #uuid "deab20e7-248d-57af-ad42-5509f60f2d31" :topic/math-academy-id 3360} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04edc601-2930-52d5-862b-aa70c1a53004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256824} {:answer/id #uuid "123bf47b-36cc-59d7-9d17-a22088107f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256948} {:answer/id #uuid "7d949795-b889-5e52-9f41-bc81ed8d37b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257892} {:answer/id #uuid "88dd0768-8c63-597d-8960-8bce308cab84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257983} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a0d9dc-f41e-5996-a270-62b9910bf70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258622} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4bee5c-5730-5fd9-a144-30c0b35fffca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8bad440-9dfc-5258-b308-3c0a302cd41a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125739" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=yz-x.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,2,0)$ to $(-2,3,1)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,2,0)$ and $B(-2,3,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(-2,3,1)-f(-1,2,0) \\\\  & =(3(1)-(-2))-(2(0)-(-1)) \\\\  & =5-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "22c4665d-8155-5ae8-8092-57bb12f99f4d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b012eb-6902-5171-a714-6e9adcc3fdee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257145} {:answer/id #uuid "729cf2e2-8525-5159-a943-e12c5d56a592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257791} {:answer/id #uuid "744ef8ea-157c-58d8-8279-46f596d5d3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257800} {:answer/id #uuid "a79e3f8e-8a0c-50b2-a4c1-c3b8e88aed24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258244} {:answer/id #uuid "d7dba790-e01b-5073-82cd-206cb42c4124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258244} :answer-field/id #uuid "82163bfb-0b92-5277-b853-9c511755cd36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "536580f0-4841-5ab4-989a-c614cdea00c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-125737" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+z.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,3)$ to $(0,-1,2)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0,3)$ and $B(0,-1,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,-1,2)-f(1,0,3) \\\\  & =({0}^{3}-(-1)^{2}+2)-({1}^{3}-{0}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "293bc725-9204-5423-bbd2-c85d97ea1144" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447006}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2f20f0-48bb-505a-a0bb-3e4578a2d1ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446999} {:answer/id #uuid "888ab4fd-bbc3-5419-bfeb-a182fae50906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447000} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a59df4-b60e-57d1-8bb8-0ef8a574c18d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447001} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ea3063-2fe4-5c0e-9794-3156030da63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447002} {:answer/id #uuid "da2b77e0-70de-507a-844b-baad5473a302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447001} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee6eda1f-7c7b-5e84-ad86-9a336560bf16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ccf7d0b-49a0-5735-9f73-94b4693e10fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-125740" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,-1,1)$ to $(2,1,0)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,-1,1)$ and $B(2,1,0).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(2,1,0)-f(0,-1,1) \\\\  & =(2(1)^{2}+{0}^{3})-(0(-1)^{2}+{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b5208760-54e7-5464-b42f-550fe3cbde8e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10794cd8-12ac-5cb8-b8c1-b910715a1350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256933} {:answer/id #uuid "2a489fae-a9d9-5967-9682-2c7dc62d72b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257155} {:answer/id #uuid "c6210f7f-4a94-5eae-829e-7c2ebbb2ff19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258514} {:answer/id #uuid "f07868a6-417c-5ea4-8b12-1af3e2b84607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258874} {:answer/id #uuid "f623dcd4-0339-5e15-b9d6-9476f7a218e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258932} :answer-field/id #uuid "28961929-f635-5865-9f98-b89b6ca896ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "088dda9c-46fc-5710-8c3d-c31a5fce7d54" :question/math-academy-id "q-125755" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=12{x}^{3}\\,i-2\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,3)$ to $(0,-1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,3)$ and $B(0,-1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=3{x}^{4}-2y.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(0,-1)-f(-1,3) \\\\  & =(3(0)^{4}-2(-1))-(3(-1)^{4}-2(3)) \\\\  & =2-(3-6) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b83ca3f6-3386-5f07-be1b-40eb7acf84d6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a30b11a-120a-5f7a-8652-df80e513b7b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256875} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3dcd67-78eb-583f-97c5-32ac61d09e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258173} {:answer/id #uuid "adfaf3dc-e92b-57ed-a5f8-0dc1710ebeb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258300} {:answer/id #uuid "af03ba57-28b4-5704-b56c-cd9a3133f7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258307} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1a8fbb-7b74-5316-bf06-61f28d3e5539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258307} :answer-field/id #uuid "075c178f-e1b3-5fc0-885f-31705c3ec578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "25a22695-171f-55bf-b6a5-1f3b602faf0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125760" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=-2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\sqrt{\\pi },1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2}-(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "da6e3be4-bbf8-5d1f-a071-2461ecbb29f8"}] :activity/title "Path Independence of Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446998}]}
